       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 20 mg daily is double the usual maximum dose of amlodipine, which is 10 mg once daily. Doses above 10 mg raise the risk of peripheral edema, hypotension, and reflex tachycardia, and they usually add little extra blood pressure control.\n\nYour patient has normal kidney and liver function and no allergies, so I don't see anything that would justify the higher dose.\n\nYou could do one of these:\n1. **Enter 10 mg PO once daily**, the maximum standard dose. If you need more blood pressure control, add a second agent such as an ACE inhibitor, an ARB, or a thiazide-like diuretic.\n2. **Start at 5 mg PO once daily** if the patient is new to amlodipine. You can titrate up after 1\u20132 weeks.\n3. **Enter 20 mg anyway** if you intend that dose, for example for a specific clinical reason or a documented plan. Please confirm and give me a note to attach, such as the rationale, and I'll enter it.\n\nWhich would you like?"
      }
